lar coordinates:\n\n1. The distance from the origin (the radius $r$) is given by:\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\]\n2. The angle $\\theta$ is given by:\n\\[ \\theta = \\begin{cases} \n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) & \\text{if } x > 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) + \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y \\ge 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) - \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y > 0 \\\\\n-\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor the point $(0, 3)$, we can see that $x = 0$ and $y = 3$. Using the formulas above:\n\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3 \\]\n\\[ \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nSo the polar coordinates of the point $(0, 3)$ are $\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$. Let's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rectangular coordinates\nx = 0\ny = 3\n\n# Calculate the radius r\nr = math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2)\n\n# Calculate the angle theta\nif x > 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x)\nelif x < 0 and y >= 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x) + math.pi\nelif x < 0 and y < 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x) - math.pi\nelif x == 0 and y > 0:\n    theta = math.pi / 2\nelif x == 0 and y < 0:\n    theta = -math.pi / 2\n\n# Ensure theta is in the range [0, 2*pi)\nif theta < 0:\n    theta += 2 * math.pi\n\nprint(((r, theta)))\n```\n```output\n(3.0, 1.5707963267948966)\n```\nThe polar coordinates of the point $(0, 3)$ are $\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}\n\\]", "To convert the rectangular coordinates $(0, 3)$ to polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, we need to use the following formulas:\n\n1. The distance from the origin (radius) $r$ is given by:\n   \\[\n   r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\n   \\]\n   where $x = 0$ and $y = 3$.\n\n2. The angle $\\theta$ is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\begin{cases} \n   \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) & \