ate the angle theta\ntheta = math.atan2(y, x)\n\n# Convert theta to the range [0, 2*pi)\nif theta < 0:\n    theta += 2 * math.pi\n\n# Display the polar coordinates\nprint(((r, theta)))\n```\n```output\n(3.0, 1.5707963267948966)\n```\nThe polar coordinates of the point $(0,3)$ are \\(\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\).\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}\n\\]", "To convert the point $(0, 3)$ from rectangular (Cartesian) coordinates to polar coordinates, we need to find the values of $r$ and $\\theta$.\n\n1. The value of $r$ (the distance from the origin to the point) can be found using the formula:\n   \\[\n   r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\n   \\]\n   For the point $(0, 3)$, $x = 0$ and $y = 3$, so:\n   \\[\n   r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n   \\]\n\n2. The value of $\\theta$ (the angle from the positive x-axis to the point) can be found using the formula:\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\begin{cases} \n   \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) & \\text{if } x > 0 \\\\\n   \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) + \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y \\ge 0 \\\\\n   \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) - \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n   \\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y > 0 \\\\\n   \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y < 0\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   For the point $(0, 3)$, $x = 0$ and $y = 3$, so:\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point $(0, 3)$ are $(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rectangular coordinates\nx = 0\ny = 3\n\n# Calculate r\nr = math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2)\n\n# Calculate theta\nif x == 0:\n    if y > 0:\n        theta = math.pi / 2\n    elif y < 0:\n        theta = 3 * math.pi / 2\n    else:\n        theta = 0\nelse:\n    theta = math.atan2(y, x)\n\n# Convert theta to be in the range [0, 2*pi)\ntheta = theta % (2 * math.pi)\n\nprint(((r, theta)))\n```\n```