n\nFor the points \\((2, -6)\\) and \\((-4, 3)\\), we can substitute \\(x_1 = 2\\), \\(y_1 = -6\\), \\(x_2 = -4\\), and \\(y_2 = 3\\) into the formula:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{((-4) - 2)^2 + (3 - (-6))^2}\n\\]\n\nLet's calculate this step by step.\n\n1. Calculate \\(x_2 - x_1\\):\n\\[\n-4 - 2 = -6\n\\]\n2. Calculate \\(y_2 - y_1\\):\n\\[\n3 - (-6) = 3 + 6 = 9\n\\]\n3. Square these differences:\n\\[\n(-6)^2 = 36\n\\]\n\\[\n9^2 = 81\n\\]\n4. Add the squares:\n\\[\n36 + 81 = 117\n\\]\n5. Take the square root of the sum:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{117} = \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 13} = \\sqrt{9} \\cdot \\sqrt{13} = 3\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nSo the distance between the points \\((2, -6)\\) and \\((-4, 3)\\) is \\(3\\sqrt{13}\\). Let's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Coordinates of the points\nx1, y1 = 2, -6\nx2, y2 = -4, 3\n\n# Calculate the differences\ndx = x2 - x1\ndy = y2 - y1\n\n# Calculate the distance using the distance formula\ndistance = math.sqrt(dx**2 + dy**2)\nprint(distance)\n```\n```output\n10.816653826391969\n```\nThe calculated distance is approximately \\(10.82\\), which matches \\(3\\sqrt{13}\\) when evaluated numerically. Therefore, the exact distance between the points \\((2, -6)\\) and \\((-4, 3)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3\\sqrt{13}}\n\\]", "To find the distance between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) in a Cartesian plane, we use the distance formula:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nGiven points are \\((2, -6)\\) and \\((-4, 3)\\). Let's substitute these points into the formula:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{((-4) - 2)^2 + (3 - (-6))^2}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying inside the square root:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + (3 + 6)^2} = \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + 9^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 81} = \\sqrt{117} = \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 13} = 3\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nSo the distance between the points \\((2, -6)\\) and \\((-4, 3)\\) is \\(3\\sqrt{13}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Coordinates of the points\nx1, y1 = 