ties\nexpanded_expr = sp.expand_trig(expr)\n\n# Extract the coefficient of sin(x)\nd = sp.collect(expanded_expr, sp.sin(x)).coeff(sp.sin(x))\n\nprint(d)\n```\n```output\n0\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the previous approach. Let's correct it by directly using the binomial expansion and simplification with SymPy.\n\nWe'll use the binomial theorem to expand \\((\\sin x)^7\\) and then collect the terms involving \\(\\sin x\\).\n\nHere is the corrected Python code:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the expression (sin(x))^7\nexpr = sp.sin(x)**7\n\n# Use trigonometric expansion\nexpr_expanded = sp.expand_trig(expr)\n\n# Collect the terms involving sin(x)\nd = sp.collect(expr_expanded, sp.sin(x)).coeff(sp.sin(x))\n\nprint(d)\n```\n```output\n0\n```\nIt appears that the previous approach did not yield the correct result. Let's take a different approach by manually expanding \\((\\sin x)^7\\) using the binomial theorem and then simplifying it.\n\nWe can use the identity \\(\\sin x = \\frac{e^{ix} - e^{-ix}}{2i}\\) and expand \\((\\sin x)^7\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n(\\sin x)^7 = \\left(\\frac{e^{ix} - e^{-ix}}{2i}\\right)^7 = \\frac{1}{(2i)^7} \\sum_{k=0}^{7} \\binom{7}{k} (e^{ix})^{7-k} (e^{-ix})^k (-1)^k.\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n(\\sin x)^7 = \\frac{1}{(2i)^7} \\sum_{k=0}^{7} \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^k e^{i(7-2k)x} = \\frac{-i}{128} \\sum_{k=0}^{7} \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^k e^{i(7-2k)x}.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the coefficient of \\(\\sin x\\), which corresponds to the term where the exponent of \\(e^{ix}\\) is 1. This happens when \\(7-2k = 1\\), or \\(k = 3\\). So, the coefficient of \\(\\sin x\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-i}{128} \\binom{7}{3} (-1)^3 = \\frac{-i}{128} \\cdot 35 \\cdot (-1) = \\frac{35i}{128}.\n\\]\n\nSince we are looking for the coefficient of \\(\\sin x\\), we take the imaginary part of this expression, which is \\(\\frac{35}{128}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with a more direct approach using SymP