inal answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, 1)}\n\\]", "To find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) such that the equation \\(f(x) - f(a) = (x - a)^2 (x - b)\\) holds, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Compute \\(f(x)\\) and \\(f(a)\\).\n2. Substitute \\(f(x)\\) and \\(f(a)\\) into the equation \\(f(x) - f(a) = (x - a)^2 (x - b)\\).\n3. Expand and simplify both sides of the equation.\n4. Compare the coefficients of the resulting polynomial equation to find \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nLet's start by computing \\(f(x)\\) and \\(f(a)\\):\n\n\\[f(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + 1\\]\n\\[f(a) = a^3 + 3a^2 + 1\\]\n\nNow substitute these into the equation:\n\n\\[f(x) - f(a) = x^3 + 3x^2 + 1 - (a^3 + 3a^2 + 1) = x^3 + 3x^2 - a^3 - 3a^2\\]\n\nThe right-hand side of the equation is:\n\n\\[(x - a)^2 (x - b) = (x^2 - 2ax + a^2)(x - b) = x^3 - bx^2 - 2ax^2 + 2abx + a^2x - a^2b = x^3 - (b + 2a)x^2 + (2ab + a^2)x - a^2b\\]\n\nNow we equate the coefficients of the polynomials on both sides:\n\n\\[x^3 + 3x^2 - a^3 - 3a^2 = x^3 - (b + 2a)x^2 + (2ab + a^2)x - a^2b\\]\n\nBy comparing the coefficients of \\(x^2\\), we get:\n\n\\[3 = -(b + 2a)\\]\n\nBy comparing the constant terms, we get:\n\n\\[-a^3 - 3a^2 = -a^2b\\]\n\nLet's solve these equations step by step. First, we solve for \\(b\\) in terms of \\(a\\) from the first equation:\n\n\\[3 = -(b + 2a) \\implies b = -3 - 2a\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\(b = -3 - 2a\\) into the second equation:\n\n\\[-a^3 - 3a^2 = -a^2(-3 - 2a) \\implies -a^3 - 3a^2 = 3a^2 + 2a^3 \\implies -a^3 - 3a^2 = 3a^2 + 2a^3 \\implies -3a^2 - 3a^2 = 2a^3 + a^3 \\implies -6a^2 = 3a^3 \\implies 3a^3 + 6a^2 = 0 \\implies 3a^2(a + 2) = 0\\]\n\nSince \\(a \\neq 0\\), we get:\n\n\\[a = -2\\]\n\nNow we substitute \\(a = -2\\) into the equation for \\(b\\):\n\n\\[b = -3 - 2(-2) = -3 + 4 = 1\\]\n\nSo the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\((-2, 1)\\). Let's verify this solution by substituting \\(a = -2\\) and \\(b = 1\\) back into the original equation to ensure it holds true.\n\nFinally, let's print the r