\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we can use the formula for the projection of vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) onto vector \\(\\mathbf{u}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{u}\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ y \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe projection is given as:\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the dot product \\(\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 1 \\cdot 2 + (-2) \\cdot y + 1 \\cdot (-5) = 2 - 2y - 5 = -2y - 3\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the dot product \\(\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 1^2 + (-2)^2 + 1^2 = 1 + 4 + 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nSo the projection formula becomes:\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{-2y - 3}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that this is equal to:\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis means:\n\\[\n\\frac{-2y - 3}{6} = \\frac{5}{6}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( y \\):\n\\[\n-2y - 3 = 5\n\\]\n\\[\n-2y = 8\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -4\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this by plugging \\( y = -4 \\) back into the projection formula and checking if we get the correct projection.\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 1 \\cdot 2 + (-2) \\cdot (-4) + 1 \\cdot (-5) = 2 + 8 - 5 = 5\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 6\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis confirms that the projection is correct when \\( y = -4 \\).\n\nThus, the value of \\( y \\) is \\(\\boxed{-4}\\).",