ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 6\\), and \\(c = -27\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 108}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{144}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm 12}{2} = 3 \\text{ or } -9\n\\]\n\nSo, the points of intersection for \\(y = 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\) are \\((3, 10 + \\sqrt{41})\\) and \\((-9, 10 + \\sqrt{41})\\).\n\nFor \\(y = 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 + (10 - \\sqrt{41})^2 + 6x - 24(10 - \\sqrt{41}) + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the constants:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + (100 - 20\\sqrt{41} + 41) + 6x - 240 + 24\\sqrt{41} + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x + 100 + 41 - 240 + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x - 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is the same quadratic equation as before, so the solutions are again \\(x = 3\\) and \\(x = -9\\). The points of intersection for \\(y = 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\) are \\((3, 10 - \\sqrt{41})\\) and \\((-9, 10 - \\sqrt{41})\\).\n\nSo, the four points of intersection are \\((3, 10 + \\sqrt{41})\\), \\((-9, 10 + \\sqrt{41})\\), \\((3, 10 - \\sqrt{41})\\), and \\((-9, 10 - \\sqrt{41})\\).\n\nNext, we need to compute the sum of the distances from these points to the point \\((-3, 2)\\). The distance formula between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is \\(\\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\\).\n\nLet's compute the distances:\n\n1. Distance from \\((3, 10 + \\sqrt{41})\\) to \\((-3, 2)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(3 - (-3))^2 + (10 + \\sqrt{41} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{(3 + 3)^2 + (8 + \\sqrt{41})^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 64 + 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{141 + 16\\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\n2. Distance from \\((-9, 10 + \\sqrt{41})\\) to \\((-3, 2)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(-9 - (-3))^2 + (10 + \\sqrt{41} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{(-9 + 3)^2 + (8 + \\sqrt{41})^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 64 + 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{141 + 16\\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\n3. Distance from \\((3, 10 - \\sqrt{41})\\) to \\((-3, 2)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(3 - (-3))^2 + (10 - \\sqrt{41} - 2)^2} = \\sqrt{(3 + 3)^2 + (8 - \\sqrt{41})^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 64 - 16\\sqrt{41} + 41} = \\sqrt{141 - 16\\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\n4. Distance from \\((-9, 10 -