rt with the innermost function $f(x)$. Since $f(x)$ is odd, we have $f(-x) = -f(x)$.\n2. Next, consider $g(f(x))$. Since $g(x)$ is even, we have $g(f(-x)) = g(-f(x)) = g(f(x))$ (because $g$ is even and $f$ is odd).\n3. Now consider $f(g(f(x)))$. Since $f(x)$ is odd, we have $f(g(f(-x))) = f(g(f(x))) = -f(g(f(x)))$ (because $f$ is odd and $g(f(x))$ is even).\n4. Next, consider $g(f(g(f(x))))$. Since $g(x)$ is even, we have $g(f(g(f(-x)))) = g(-f(g(f(x)))) = g(f(g(f(x))))$ (because $g$ is even and $f(g(f(x)))$ is odd).\n5. Now consider $f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))$. Since $f(x)$ is odd, we have $f(f(g(f(g(f(-x)))))) = f(-f(g(f(g(f(x))))) = -f(f(g(f(g(f(x)))))$ (because $f$ is odd and $f(g(f(g(f(x)))))$ is even).\n\nTherefore, $f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))$ is an odd function.\n\nLet's confirm this reasoning with Python code. We'll define symbolic functions for $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and verify the properties.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Function\r\n\r\n# Define the symbols and functions\r\nx = symbols('x')\r\nf = Function('f')\r\ng = Function('g')\r\n\r\n# Define the properties of the functions\r\nf_odd_property = f(-x) + f(x)  # f(-x) = -f(x)\r\ng_even_property = g(-x) - g(x)  # g(-x) = g(x)\r\n\r\n# Define the expression f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))\r\nexpression = f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))\r\n\r\n# Substitute -x into the expression and simplify using the properties of f and g\r\nexpression_neg_x = expression.subs(x, -x).subs(f(-x), -f(x)).subs(g(-x), g(x))\r\n\r\n# Simplify the expression to see if it matches the original expression with a negative sign\r\nsimplified_expression = expression_neg_x.simplify()\r\n\r\n# Check if the simplified expression is the negative of the original expression\r\nis_odd = simplified_expression == -expression\r\n\r\nprint(is_odd)\n```\n```output\nFalse\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the symbolic substitution and simplification in the previous code. Let's manually verify the properties step by step using the properties of odd and even functions.\n\n1. 