trix. The coordinates of \\(D\\) are \\((0,1)\\). After rotating by \\( \\frac{900^\\circ}{7} \\), the new coordinates of \\(C\\) will be:\n     \\[\n     C = \\left( \\cos\\left(\\frac{900^\\circ}{7}\\right), \\sin\\left(\\frac{900^\\circ}{7}\\right) \\right)\n     \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the Angle \\( \\angle BAC \\):**\n   - The angle \\( \\angle BAC \\) can be found using the dot product of vectors \\( \\overrightarrow{AB} \\) and \\( \\overrightarrow{AC} \\). The vector \\( \\overrightarrow{AB} \\) is \\((1,0)\\) and the vector \\( \\overrightarrow{AC} \\) is \\( \\left( \\cos\\left(\\frac{900^\\circ}{7}\\right), \\sin\\left(\\frac{900^\\circ}{7}\\right) \\right) \\).\n   - The dot product of \\( \\overrightarrow{AB} \\) and \\( \\overrightarrow{AC} \\) is \\( 1 \\cdot \\cos\\left(\\frac{900^\\circ}{7}\\right) + 0 \\cdot \\sin\\left(\\frac{900^\\circ}{7}\\right) = \\cos\\left(\\frac{900^\\circ}{7}\\right) \\).\n   - The magnitudes of the vectors are \\(1\\) and \\(1\\), so the cosine of the angle \\( \\angle BAC \\) is \\( \\cos\\left(\\frac{900^\\circ}{7}\\right) \\). Therefore, the angle \\( \\angle BAC \\) is \\( \\frac{900^\\circ}{7} - 90^\\circ \\).\n\nLet's calculate this using Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angle of the heptagon\nheptagon_angle = sp.Rational(900, 7)\n\n# Calculate the angle BAC\nangle_BAC = heptagon_angle - 90\n\n# Simplify the angle\nangle_BAC_simplified = sp.simplify(angle_BAC)\n\nprint(angle_BAC_simplified)\n```\n```output\n270/7\n```\nThe degree measure of angle \\( \\angle BAC \\) is \\( \\frac{270^\\circ}{7} \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{270}{7}}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to understand the geometric properties of the square and the regular heptagon, and how they interact when they share a common side.\n\n1. **Properties of the Square**: Each internal angle of a square is \\(90^\\circ\\). The side length of the square is the same as the side length of the heptagon.\n\n2. **Properties of the Regular Heptagon**: Each internal angle of a regular heptagon is \\(\\frac{(7-2) \\times 180^\\circ}{7} = \\frac{5 \\times 180^\\circ}{7} = \\frac{900^\\circ}{7}\\). The side length of the heptagon is the same as the side length of the square.\n\n3. **Angle Calculation**: When the square and heptagon share a common side \\(\\overline{AD}\\), the angle \\(\\angle BAD\\) is composed of the internal angle of the square and the internal angle of the heptagon. The angle \\(\\angle BAC\\) is the difference between the internal angle of the heptagon and the internal angle of the square.\n\nLet's denote the internal angle of the heptagon as \\(\\theta\\). Then \\(\\theta = \\frac{900^\\circ}{7}\\). The internal angle of the square is \\(90^\\circ\\). The angle \\(\\angle BAC\\) is the difference between \\(\\theta\\) and \\(90^\\circ\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAC = \\theta - 90^\\circ = \\frac{900^\\circ}{7} - 90^\\circ = \\frac{900^\\circ - 630^\\circ}{7} = \\frac{270^\\circ}{7}\n\\]\n\nSo, the degree measure of angle \\(\\angle BAC\\) is \\(\\frac{270^\\circ}{7}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\n# Calculate the internal angle of the heptagon\ninternal_angle_heptagon = (7 - 2) * 180 / 7\n\n# The internal angle of the square\ninternal_angle_square = 90\n\n# Calculate the angle BAC\nangle_BAC = internal_angle_heptagon - internal_angle_square\nprint(angle_BAC)\n```\n```output\n38.571428571428584\n```\nThe degree measure of angle \\(\\angle BAC\\) is \\(\\frac{270^\\circ}{7}\\), which is approximately \\(38.571428571428584^\\circ\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{270}{7}}\n\\]\n\nThe graph of the function $y = -1 + \\log_a(x+3)$ ($a>0$ and $a \\neq 1$) always passes through a fixed point A. If point A lies on the line $mx + ny + 1 = 0$, where both $m$ and $n$ are greater than 0, then the minimum value of $\\frac{1}{m} + \\frac{2}{n}$ is \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_.\nTo solve this problem, l