les of \\(90^\\circ\\).\n   - A regular heptagon has internal angles of \\( \\frac{(7-2) \\cdot 180^\\circ}{7} = \\frac{5 \\cdot 180^\\circ}{7} = \\frac{900^\\circ}{7} \\approx 128.57^\\circ \\).\n\n2. **Identify the Angle \\( \\angle BAD \\):**\n   - Since \\( \\overline{AD} \\) is a common side, the angle \\( \\angle BAD \\) is the internal angle of the square minus the internal angle of the heptagon at point \\(A\\). So, \\( \\angle BAD = 90^\\circ - \\frac{900^\\circ}{7} \\).\n\n3. **Calculate \\( \\angle BAC \\):**\n   - The angle \\( \\angle BAC \\) is the same as \\( \\angle BAD \\) because \\( \\overline{AD} \\) is a straight line and the angles are adjacent.\n\nLet's calculate the exact value of \\( \\angle BAC \\) using Python and sympy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the internal angle of the heptagon\nheptagon_angle = sp.Rational(900, 7)\n\n# Calculate the angle BAD\nangle_BAD = 90 - heptagon_angle\n\n# The angle BAC is the same as angle BAD\nangle_BAC = angle_BAD\n\n# Print the result\nprint(angle_BAC)\n```\n```output\n-450/7\n```\nIt seems there was a misunderstanding in the calculation. The angle \\( \\angle BAD \\) should be calculated correctly as the difference between the internal angle of the square and the internal angle of the heptagon. However, since the internal angle of the heptagon is greater than 90 degrees, we need to consider the correct geometric relationship.\n\nThe correct approach is to recognize that the angle \\( \\angle BAC \\) is actually the supplementary angle to \\( \\angle BAD \\) because the internal angle of the heptagon at point \\(A\\) is greater than 90 degrees. So, we need to calculate the angle as follows:\n\n\\[ \\angle BAC = 180^\\circ - \\left(90^\\circ + \\left(\\frac{900^\\circ}{7} - 90^\\circ\\right)\\right) = 180^\\circ - \\frac{900^\\circ}{7} \\]\n\nLet's correct the calculation using Python and sympy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the internal angle of the heptagon\nheptagon_angle 