 \\) such that the total number of squares is 70.\n\nLet's write the Python code to solve this problem using sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable n\nn = sp.symbols('n')\n\n# Define the equations for the number of squares\nnum_1x1_squares = 3 * (n - 1)\nnum_2x2_squares = 3 * (n - 2)\nnum_3x3_squares = 3 * (n - 3)\n\n# Total number of squares\ntotal_squares = num_1x1_squares + num_2x2_squares + num_3x3_squares\n\n# Set up the equation to solve\nequation = sp.Eq(total_squares, 70)\n\n# Solve for n\nsolution = sp.solve(equation, n)\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n[25/3]\n```\nThe output \\(\\frac{25}{3}\\) suggests that \\(n\\) is not an integer, which doesn't make sense in the context of the problem since \\(n\\) must be an integer representing the number of columns in the grid. \n\nLet's re-examine the problem and the equations we set up. The issue might be in the formulation of the equations for the number of squares. \n\nWe need to ensure that we correctly count the squares of each size in a \\(3 \\times n\\) grid. Let's re-evaluate the number of squares for each size:\n\n1. **Number of \\(1 \\times 1\\) squares:**\n   - Each \\(1 \\times 1\\) square can be placed in any of the \\(3 \\times (n-1)\\) positions.\n\n2. **Number of \\(2 \\times 2\\) squares:**\n   - Each \\(2 \\times 2\\) square can be placed in any of the \\(3 \\times (n-2)\\) positions.\n\n3. **Number of \\(3 \\times 3\\) squares:**\n   - Each \\(3 \\times 3\\) square can be placed in any of the \\(3 \\times (n-3)\\) positions.\n\nThe total number of squares is the sum of these counts:\n\\[ 3(n-1) + 3(n-2) + 3(n-3) = 70 \\]\n\nLet's re-write the Python code to solve this problem correctly:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable n\nn = sp.symbols('n')\n\n# Define the equations for the number of squares\nnum_1x1_squares = 3 * (n - 1)\nnum_2x2_squares = 3 * (n - 2)\nnum_3x3_squares = 3 * (n - 3)\n\n# Total number of squares\ntotal_squares = num_1x1_squares