ression\r\nexpr = sqrt(2) + 1/sqrt(2) + sqrt(3) + 1/sqrt(3)\r\n\r\n# Simplify the expression\r\nsimplified_expr = simplify(expr)\r\n\r\n# Convert the expression to a common denominator\r\ncommon_denominator = 6\r\nnumerator = simplified_expr.as_numer_denom()[0] * common_denominator / simplified_expr.as_numer_denom()[1]\r\n\r\n# Extract the coefficients of sqrt(2) and sqrt(3)\r\na = numerator.coeff(sqrt(2))\r\nb = numerator.coeff(sqrt(3))\r\nc = common_denominator\r\n\r\n# Calculate a + b + c\r\nresult = a + b + c\r\nprint((simplified_expr, a, b, c, result))\n```\n```output\n(sqrt(3) + sqrt(2) + 5*sqrt(6)/6, 0, 0, 6, 6)\n```\nIt appears that the expression was not correctly simplified to the form we expected. Let's manually confirm the simplification again and then use Python to extract the coefficients correctly.\n\nWe'll manually simplify the expression as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{2} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + \\sqrt{3} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\sqrt{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\sqrt{3} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\n\nCombining the terms:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{2}}{2} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{3} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{9\\sqrt{2} + 8\\sqrt{3}}{6}\n\\]\n\nThis matches the form \\(\\frac{a\\sqrt{2} + b\\sqrt{3}}{c}\\) where \\(a = 9\\), \\(b = 8\\), and \\(c = 6\\).\n\nFinally, we compute \\(a + b + c\\):\n\n\\[\na + b + c = 9 + 8 + 6 = 23\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code again, ensuring we correctly extract the coefficients.\n```python\nfrom sympy import sqrt, Rational, simplify\r\n\r\n# Define the expression\r\nexpr = sqrt(2) + Rational(1, 2)*sqrt(2) + sqrt(3) + Rational(1, 3)*sqrt(3)\r\n\r\n# Simplify the expression\r\nsimplified_expr = simplify(expr)\r\n\r\n# Convert the expression to a common denominator\r\ncommon_denominator = 6\r\nn