 = 6 / 6\nprint(k)\n```\n```output\n1.0\n```\nThe value of \\( k \\) is indeed \\( 1 \\). Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]", "To determine how many positive integers \\( n > 1 \\) make \\( 2^{24} \\) a perfect \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) power, we need to understand the conditions under which \\( 2^{24} \\) is a perfect \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) power. For \\( 2^{24} \\) to be a perfect \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) power, \\( 24 \\) must be divisible by \\( n \\). This is because \\( 2^{24} \\) can be written as \\( (2^{24/n})^n \\), and \\( 24/n \\) must be an integer.\n\nSo, we need to find all the divisors of 24 that are greater than 1. The divisors of 24 are \\( 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24 \\). Since we are looking for \\( n > 1 \\), we exclude 1. Therefore, the valid divisors are \\( 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24 \\).\n\nLet's count these divisors using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Finding all divisors of 24 that are greater than 1\ndef find_divisors(n):\n    divisors = []\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            divisors.append(i)\n    return divisors\n\n# Number 24\nnumber = 24\ndivisors = find_divisors(number)\nprint((divisors, len(divisors)))\n```\n```output\n([2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24], 7)\n```\nThe divisors of 24 that are greater than 1 are \\(2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24\\). There are 7 such divisors. Therefore, the number of positive integers \\( n > 1 \\) for which \\( 2^{24} \\) is a perfect \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) power is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).", "To determine how many positive integers \\( n > 1 \\) make \\( 2^{24} \\) a perfect \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) power, we need to find the values of \\( n \\) for which \\( 2^{24} \\) can be expressed as \\( (2^k)^n \\) for some integer \\( k \\). This means that \\( 24 \\) must be divisible by \\( n \\). So, we need to find the divisors of \\( 24 \\) that are greater than \\( 1 \\).\n\nThe divisors of \\( 24 \\) are \\( 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24 \\). Since we are looking for \\( n > 1 \\), 