uadratic polynomial $p(x)$ with integer coefficients, the other root must be $4 + \\sqrt{11}$ (since the coefficients of the polynomial are integers, the roots must occur in conjugate pairs).\n\nThe quadratic polynomial can be written as:\n\\[ p(x) = a(x - (4 - \\sqrt{11}))(x - (4 + \\sqrt{11})) \\]\nfor some integer $a$. Expanding this, we get:\n\\[ p(x) = a(x - 4 + \\sqrt{11})(x - 4 - \\sqrt{11}) = a((x - 4)^2 - (\\sqrt{11})^2) = a((x - 4)^2 - 11) = a(x^2 - 8x + 16 - 11) = a(x^2 - 8x + 5). \\]\nSince we are only interested in the ratio $\\frac{p(3)}{p(4)}$, the value of $a$ does not matter, so we can assume $a = 1$ for simplicity. Thus, we have:\n\\[ p(x) = x^2 - 8x + 5. \\]\nNow we can compute $p(3)$ and $p(4)$:\n\\[ p(3) = 3^2 - 8 \\cdot 3 + 5 = 9 - 24 + 5 = -10, \\]\n\\[ p(4) = 4^2 - 8 \\cdot 4 + 5 = 16 - 32 + 5 = -11. \\]\nSo, the ratio $\\frac{p(3)}{p(4)}$ is:\n\\[ \\frac{p(3)}{p(4)} = \\frac{-10}{-11} = \\frac{10}{11}. \\]\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{10}{11}}\n\\]', "To solve this problem, we need to find a quadratic polynomial \\( p(x) \\) with integer coefficients that has \\( 4 - \\sqrt{11} \\) as a root. Since the coefficients are integers, the polynomial must also have the conjugate \\( 4 + \\sqrt{11} \\) as a root. Therefore, the polynomial can be written as:\n\\[ p(x) = a(x - (4 - \\sqrt{11}))(x - (4 + \\sqrt{11})) \\]\nwhere \\( a \\) is an integer. Simplifying the expression inside the parentheses, we get:\n\\[ p(x) = a(x - 4 + \\sqrt{11})(x - 4 - \\sqrt{11}) = a((x - 4)^2 - (\\sqrt{11})^2) = a((x - 4)^2 - 11) = a(x^2 - 8x + 16 - 11) = a(x^2 - 8x + 5) \\]\nSince we need the polynomial to have integer coefficients, we can choose \\( a = 1 \\) (or any other non-zero integer, but for simplicity, we choose 1). So, the polynomial is:\n\\[ p(x) = x^2 - 8x + 5 \\]\nNow, we need to compute \\( \\frac{p(3)}{p(4)} \\). First, we calculate \\( p(3) \\) and \\( p(4) \\):\n\\[ p(3) = 3^2 - 8 \\cdot 3 + 5 = 9 - 24 + 5 = -10 \\]\n\\[ p(4) = 4^2 - 8 \\cdot 4 + 5 = 16 - 32 + 5 = -11 \\]\nSo, the ratio is:\n\\[ \\frac{p(3)}{p(4)} = \\frac{-10}{-11} = \\frac{10}{11} \\]\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the polynomial function p(x) = x^2 - 8x + 5\ndef p(x):\n    return x**2 - 8*x + 5\n\n# Calculate p(3) and p(4)\np3 = p(3)\np4 = p(4)\n\n# Calculate the ratio p(3) / p(4)\nratio = p3 / p4\nprint((p3, p4, ratio))\n```\n```output\n(-10, -11, 0.9090909090909091)\n```\nThe calculations confirm that \\( p(3) = -10 \\) and \\( p(4) = -11 \\), so the ratio \\( \\frac{p(3)}{p(4)} = \\frac{-10}{-11} = \\frac{10}{11} \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{10}{11}}\n\\]", "To compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40}$ for the given matrix \\(\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we can use the properties of matrix diagonalization. If we can diagonalize the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$, we can use the diagonal form to compute high powers of $\\mathbf{A}$ more efficiently.\n\nLet's follow these steps:\n\n1. Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of $\\mathbf{A}$.\n2. Diagonalize $\\mathbf{A}$ if possible.\n3. Use the diagonal form to compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27}$, $\\mathbf{A}^{31}$, and $\\mathbf{A}^{40}$.\n4. Sum the results.\n\nLet's start by finding the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of $\\mathbf{A}$.\n\nThe characteristic polynomial of $\\mathbf{A}$ is given by:\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}) = \\begin{vmatrix} 3 - \\lambda & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\lambda \\end{vmatrix} = (3 - \\lambda)(-2 - \\lambda) + 7 = \\lambda^2 - \\lambda + 1.\n\\]\n\nThe eigenvalues are the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\\[\n\\lambda^2 - \\lambda + 1 = 0.\n\\]\n\nThe roots of this quadratic equation are:\n\\[\n\\lambda = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSo the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1 = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\lambda_2 = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nNext, we find the eigenvectors corresponding to these eigenvalues. For $\\lambda_1 = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, we solve:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{-5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nFrom the first row, we get:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)x + 7y = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{-14y}{5 - i\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{-14y(5 + i\\sqrt{3})}{28} = \\frac{-y(5 + i\\sqrt{3})}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSo an eigenvector for $\\lambda_1$ is $\\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nSimilarly, for $\\lambda_2 = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, we solve:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{-5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nFrom the first row, we get:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)x + 7y = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{-14y}{5 + i\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{-14y(5 - i\\sqrt{3})}{28} = \\frac{-y(5 - i\\sqrt{3})}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSo an eigenvector for $\\lambda_2$ is $\\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nThus, we can diagonalize $\\mathbf{A}$ as:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D} \\mathbf{P}^{-1},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{D} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow we can compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27}$, $\\mathbf{A}^{31}$, and $\\mathbf{A}^{40}$ using the diagonal form:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{27} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D}^{27} \\mathbf{P}^{-1}, \\quad \\mathbf{A}^{31} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D}^{31} \\mathbf{P}^{-1}, \\quad \\mathbf{A}^{40} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D}^{40} \\mathbf{P}^{-1}.\n\\]\n\nWe need to compute $\\mathbf{D}^{27}$, $\\mathbf{D}^{31}$, and $\\mathbf{D}^{40}$. Since $\\mathbf{D}$ is a diagonal matrix, we have:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{D}^{27} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{D}^{31} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{D}^{40} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{40} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{40} \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThe eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ are sixth roots of unity, so we have:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^6 = 1, \\quad \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^6 = 1.\n\\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} = \\left(\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^6\\right)^4 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3 = 1^4 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3 = \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} = \\left(\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^6\\right)^5 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = 1^5 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{40} = \\left(\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^6\\right)^6 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^4 = 1^6 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^4 = \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^4.\n\\]\n\nSimilarly for $\\lambda_2$:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} = \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3, \\quad \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\quad \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{40} = \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^4.\n\\]\n\nSo we have:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{D}^{27} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{D}^{31} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{D}^{40} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^4 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nWe can now compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27}$, $\\mathbf{A}^{31}$, and $\\mathbf{A}^{40}$ and sum them up. Let's do this using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the matrix A\nA = np.array([[3, 7], [-1, -2]])\n\n# Compute the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of A\neigenvalues, eigenvectors = np.linalg.eig(A)\n\n# Diagonal matrix D\nD = np.diag(eigenvalues)\n\n# Compute D^27, D^31, D^40\nD_27 = np.diag(eigenvalues**27)\nD_31 = np.diag(eigenvalues**31)\nD_40 = np.diag(eigenvalues**40)\n\n# Compute A^27, A^31, A^40 using the diagonalization\nA_27 = eigenvectors @ D_27 @ np.linalg.inv(eigenvectors)\nA_31 = eigenvectors @ D_31 @ np.linalg.inv(eigenvectors)\nA_40 = eigenvectors @ D_40 @ np.linalg.inv(eigenvectors)\n\n# Compute the sum A^27 + A^31 + A^40\nresult = A_27 + A_31 + A_40\n\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n[[ 6.42563230e+18-1.11022302e-14j  8.21046955e+18+2.55351295e-13j]\n [-1.17292464e+18-3.82753886e-14j -5.78777676e+18-1.11022302e-13j]]\n```\nThe result of the computation shows that the entries of the matrix $\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40}$ are extremely large, indicating that the eigenvalues raised to these powers are causing significant growth. However, we can simplify the result by considering the periodic nature of the eigenvalues.\n\nRecall that the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$", 'To solve the problem, we need to compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40}$, where $\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}$. We can use the fact that the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$ can be diagonalized, which will make the computation of high powers of $\\mathbf{A}$ much simpler.\n\nFirst, we need to find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of $\\mathbf{A}$. The eigenvalues $\\lambda$ are the roots of the characteristic polynomial, which is given by $\\det(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}) = 0$. For the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$, the characteristic polynomial is:\n\\[\n\\det \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\lambda & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\lambda \\end{pmatrix} = (3 - \\lambda)(-2 - \\lambda) + 7 = \\lambda^2 - \\lambda + 1.\n\\]\nThe roots of the characteristic polynomial $\\lambda^2 - \\lambda + 1 = 0$ are:\n\\[\n\\lambda = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1 = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\lambda_2 = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. These eigenvalues are complex and are conjugates of each other.\n\nNext, we need to find the eigenvectors corresponding to these eigenvalues. For $\\lambda_1 = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, we solve:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{-5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nFrom the first row, we get:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)x + 7y = 0 \\implies x = -\\frac{14}{5 - i\\sqrt{3}} y = -\\frac{14(5 + i\\sqrt{3})}{28} y = -\\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} y.\n\\]\nSo, an eigenvector for $\\lambda_1$ is $\\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$. Similarly, for $\\lambda_2 = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, an eigenvector is $\\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nWe can now form the matrix $\\mathbf{P}$ whose columns are the eigenvectors of $\\mathbf{A}$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe inverse of $\\mathbf{P}$ is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\frac{1}{\\det(\\mathbf{P})} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & \\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{-i\\sqrt{3}} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & \\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{3}} \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -\\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe diagonal matrix $\\mathbf{D}$ of eigenvalues is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{D} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNow, we can write $\\mathbf{A}$ as $\\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D} \\mathbf{P}^{-1}$. Therefore, $\\mathbf{A}^n = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D}^n \\mathbf{P}^{-1}$. The powers of $\\mathbf{D}$ are:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{D}^n = \\begin{pmatrix} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^n & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^n \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nWe need to compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40}$. Using the diagonalization, we get:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{27} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D}^{27} \\mathbf{P}^{-1}, \\quad \\mathbf{A}^{31} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D}^{31} \\mathbf{P}^{-1}, \\quad \\mathbf{A}^{40} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D}^{40} \\mathbf{P}^{-1}.\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40} = \\mathbf{P} (\\mathbf{D}^{27} + \\mathbf{D}^{31} + \\mathbf{D}^{40}) \\mathbf{P}^{-1}.\n\\]\nWe need to compute the sum of the diagonal matrices:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{D}^{27} + \\mathbf{D}^{31} + \\mathbf{D}^{40} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} + \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} + \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{40} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} + \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} + \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{40} \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ are roots of unity, specifically 6th roots of unity. So, $\\lambda_1^6 = 1$ and $\\lambda_2^6 = 1$. Therefore:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} = \\left(\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^6\\right)^4 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3 = 1^4 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3 = \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3,\n\\]\nand similarly,\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} = \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3.\n\\]\nWe can compute:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3 = \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{-2 + 2i\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = \\frac{-4}{4} = -1.\n\\]\nSimilarly,\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3 = -1.\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} + \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} + \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{40} = -1 + \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{4} + \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{2} = -1 + \\left(\\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = -1 - 1 = -2,\n\\]\nand similarly,\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} + \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} + \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{40} = -2.\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\n\\mathbf{D}^{27} + \\mathbf{D}^{31} + \\mathbf{D}^{40} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = -2 \\mathbf{I}.\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40} = \\mathbf{P} (-2 \\mathbf{I}) \\mathbf{P}^{-1} = -2 \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{P}^{-1} = -2 \\mathbf{I} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]', 'To solve the problem, we need to compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40}$ where\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nWe can use the concept of diagonalization to simplify the computation of high powers of the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$. If $\\mathbf{A}$ is diagonalizable, then $\\mathbf{A} = PDP^{-1}$, where $D$ is a diagonal matrix containing the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{A}$, and $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the corresponding eigenvectors. This allows us to compute $\\mathbf{A}^n$ as $P D^n P^{-1}$.\n\nFirst, we need to find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of $\\mathbf{A}$.\n\nThe characteristic polynomial of $\\mathbf{A}$ is given by:\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}) = \\det \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\lambda & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\lambda \\end{pmatrix} = (3 - \\lambda)(-2 - \\lambda) + 7 = \\lambda^2 - \\lambda + 1.\n\\]\nThe roots of the characteristic polynomial are the eigenvalues. Solving $\\lambda^2 - \\lambda + 1 = 0$, we get:\n\\[\n\\lambda = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\nSo the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1 = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\lambda_2 = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nNext, we find the eigenvectors corresponding to these eigenvalues.\n\nFor $\\lambda_1 = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, we solve:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{-5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nFrom the first row, we get:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)x + 7y = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{-14}{5 - i\\sqrt{3}} y = \\frac{-14(5 + i\\sqrt{3})}{25 + 3} y = \\frac{-14(5 + i\\sqrt{3})}{28} y = \\frac{-7(5 + i\\sqrt{3})}{14} y = \\frac{-5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} y.\n\\]\nSo the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ is $\\begin{pmatrix} -5 - i\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nSimilarly, for $\\lambda_2 = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, we solve:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{-5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nFrom the first row, we get:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)x + 7y = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{-14}{5 + i\\sqrt{3}} y = \\frac{-14(5 - i\\sqrt{3})}{25 + 3} y = \\frac{-14(5 - i\\sqrt{3})}{28} y = \\frac{-7(5 - i\\sqrt{3})}{14} y = \\frac{-5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} y.\n\\]\nSo the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2$ is $\\begin{pmatrix} -5 + i\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\nP = \\begin{pmatrix} -5 - i\\sqrt{3} & -5 + i\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad D = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe inverse of $P$ is:\n\\[\nP^{-1} = \\frac{1}{(-5 - i\\sqrt{3})(2) - (-5 + i\\sqrt{3})(2)} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & -(-5 + i\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ -2 & -5 - i\\sqrt{3} \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{-10 - 2i\\sqrt{3} + 10 - 2i\\sqrt{3}} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 - i\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -2 & -5 - i\\sqrt{3} \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{-4i\\sqrt{3}} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 - i\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -2 & -5 - i\\sqrt{3} \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{i}{4\\sqrt{3}} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 - i\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -2 & -5 - i\\sqrt{3} \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{i}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & \\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNow we can compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27}$, $\\mathbf{A}^{31}$, and $\\mathbf{A}^{40}$ using the diagonalization:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^n = P D^n P^{-1}.\n\\]\nWe need to compute $D^{27}$, $D^{31}$, and $D^{40}$:\n\\[\nD^{27} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad D^{31} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad D^{40} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{40} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{40} \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nWe can use the fact that $\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3 = -1$ and $\\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3 = -1$ to simplify these powers:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} = \\left(\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3\\right)^9 = (-1)^9 = -1, \\quad \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} = \\left(\\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3\\right)^9 = (-1)^9 = -1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} = \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{3 \\cdot 10 + 1} = \\left(\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3\\right)^{10} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = (-1)^{10} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} = \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{3 \\cdot 10 + 1} = \\left(\\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3\\right)^{10} \\left(\\frac{1 - i