bf{0}$. For $\\lambda_1 = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, we solve:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{-5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nWe can choose $v_1 = 2$ and $v_2 = \\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{7}$, so the eigenvector is $\\mathbf{v}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ \\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{7} \\end{pmatrix}$. Similarly, for $\\lambda_2 = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, we solve:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{-5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nWe can choose $v_1 = 2$ and $v_2 = \\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{7}$, so the eigenvector is $\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ \\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{7} \\end{pmatrix}$. Thus, the matrix $\\mathbf{P}$ is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 2 \\\\ \\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{7} & \\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{7} \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe inverse of $\\mathbf{P}$ is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\frac{1}{2 \\cdot \\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{7} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{7}} \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{7} & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{7} & 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{7}{2i\\sqrt{3}} \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{7} & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{7} & 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{i\\sqrt{3}} \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{7} & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{7} & 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNow we can compute $\\