bs(x, critical_point)\nprint(max_value)\n```\n```output\n81\n```\nThe maximum value of the expression \\(4(x + 7)(2 - x)\\) is \\(\\boxed{81}\\).", "To find the maximum value of the expression \\(4(x + 7)(2 - x)\\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Expand the expression to get a standard quadratic form.\n2. Find the vertex of the quadratic equation, which will give us the maximum value since the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) is negative (indicating a downward-opening parabola).\n\nLet's start by expanding the expression:\n\n\\[4(x + 7)(2 - x) = 4(x \\cdot 2 + x \\cdot (-x) + 7 \\cdot 2 + 7 \\cdot (-x)) = 4(2x - x^2 + 14 - 7x) = 4(-x^2 - 5x + 14) = -4x^2 - 20x + 56.\\]\n\nNow we have the quadratic expression in the form \\(ax^2 + bx + c\\), where \\(a = -4\\), \\(b = -20\\), and \\(c = 56\\).\n\nThe vertex form for a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c\\) occurs at \\(x = -\\frac{b}{2a}\\). So, we can find the value of \\(x\\) that maximizes the expression:\n\n\\[x = -\\frac{-20}{2 \\cdot -4} = -\\frac{-20}{-8} = -\\frac{5}{2}.\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\(x = -\\frac{5}{2}\\) back into the expression to find the maximum value:\n\n\\[-4\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2 - 20\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right) + 56 = -4\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) + 50 + 56 = -25 + 50 + 56 = 81.\\]\n\nSo, the maximum value of the expression is 81. Let's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the variable and the expression\r\nx = sp.symbols('x')\r\nexpression = 4 * (x + 7) * (2 - x)\r\n\r\n# Expand the expression\r\nexpanded_expression = sp.expand(expression)\r\n\r\n# Find the vertex (maximum point) of the quadratic expression\r\na = expanded_expression.coeff(x, 2)\r\nb = expanded_expression.coeff(x, 1)\r\nc = expanded_expression.coeff(x, 0)\r\n\r\n# The x-coordinate of the vertex is -b/(2a)\r\nx_max = -b / (2 * a)\r\n\r\n# Substitute x_max back into the expression to find the maximum value\r\nmax_value = expanded_expression.subs(x, x_max)\r\nprint(max_value)\n