 \\sqrt{r}) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\\[ x \\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(1 + r)} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\right)} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(1 + 2r)} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(1 + \\sqrt{r})} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right)} \\]\n\nSince \\(\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\) is a constant, the value of \\(x\\) is inversely proportional to the natural logarithm of the base. The base with the smallest natural logarithm will give the largest value of \\(x\\). Let's compare the natural logarithms of the bases:\n\n\\[ \\ln(1 + r) \\]\n\\[ \\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\right) \\]\n\\[ \\ln(1 + 2r) \\]\n\\[ \\ln(1 + \\sqrt{r}) \\]\n\\[ \\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right) \\]\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), the smallest natural logarithm will be \\(\\ln(1 + r)\\) because \\(1 + r\\) is the smallest base among the given options. Therefore, the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is the one with the base \\(1 + r\\), which is option (A).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and the constant\nr = sp.symbols('r', positive=True, real=True)\nln_7_over_3 = sp.ln(7/3)\n\n# Define the expressions for x for each equation\nx_A = ln_7_over_3 / sp.ln(1 + r)\nx_B = ln_7_over_3 / sp.ln(1 + r/10)\nx_C = ln_7_over_3 / sp.ln(1 + 2*r)\nx_D = ln_7_over_3 / sp.ln(1 + sp.sqrt(r))\nx_E = ln_7_over_3 / sp.ln(1 + 1/r)\n\n# Compare the expressions\ncomparison = {\n    'A': x_A,\n    'B': x_B,\n    'C': x_C,\n    'D': x_D,\n    'E': x_E\n}\n\n# Find the maximum value of x\nmax_x = max(comparison, key=lambda k: comparison[k].subs(r, 1))  # Substitute r with 1 to compare the expressions\nprint(max_x)\n```\n```output\nA\n```\nThe output confirms that the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is option (A), \\(3(1 + r)^x = 7\\).\n\nTherefore, the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{A}\\).", "To determine which equation has the largest solution \\(x\\), we need to solve each equation for \\(x\\) and compare the solutions. We can use logarithms to isolate \\(x\\) in each equation. Let's start by solving each equation for \\(x\\).\n\n1. For equation \\(\\textbf{(A)}\\), \\(3(1 + r)^x = 7\\):\n\\[\n(1 + r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + r)}\n\\]\n\n2. For equation \\(\\textbf{(B)}\\), \\(3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7\\):\n\\[\n(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + \\frac{r}{10})}\n\\]\n\n3. For equation \\(\\textbf{(C)}\\), \\(3(1 + 2r)^x = 7\\):\n\\[\n(1 + 2r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + 2r)}\n\\]\n\n4. For equation \\(\\textbf{(D)}\\), \\(3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7\\):\n\\[\n(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + \\sqrt{r})}\n\\]\n\n5. For equation \\(\\textbf{(E)}\\), \\(3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7\\):\n\\[\n(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + \\frac{1}{r})}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\) is a constant, the value of \\(x\\) is inversely proportional to \\(\\log(1 + \\text{some expression involving } r)\\). The smaller the value of \\(\\log(1 + \\text{some expression involving } r)\\), the larger the value of \\(x\\). Therefore, we need to compare the values of \\(\\log(1 + r)\\), \\(\\log(1 + \\frac{r}{10})\\), \\(\\log(1 + 2r)\\), \\(\\log(1 + \\sqrt{r})\\), and \\(\\log(1 + \\frac{1}{r})\\).\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), we can analyze the expressions inside the logarithms:\n\n- \\(1 + r\\)\n- \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\)\n- \\(1 + 2r\\)\n- \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\)\n- \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\)\n\nClearly, \\(1 + 2r\\) is the largest among these expressions because \\(2r > r\\) and \\(2r > \\sqrt{r}\\) for \\(0 < r < 3\\). Therefore, \\(\\log(1 + 2r)\\) is the largest among the logarithms, and consequently, \\(\\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + 2r)}\\) is the smallest.\n\nThus, the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is \\(\\textbf{(C)}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this by calculating the values using Python code for a specific \\(r\\) in the range \\(0 < r < 3\\), for example, \\(r = 1\\).\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Define the value of r\nr = 1\n\n# Calculate the values of x for each equation\nx_A = math.log(7/3) / math.log(1 + r)\nx_B = math.log(7/3) / math.log(1 + r/10)\nx_C = math.log(7/3) / math.log(1 + 2*r)\nx_D = math.log(7/3) / math.log(1 + math.sqrt(r))\nx_E = math.log(7/3) / math.log(1 + 1/r)\n\nprint(((x_A, x_B, x_C, x_D, x_E)))\n```\n```output\n(1.2052428582545622, 1.2689653824235076, 1.0691582850574766, 1.1221762088633366, 0.8289210206656966)\n```\nThe calculated values of \\(x\\) for each equation are as follows:\n- \\(x_A \\approx 1.205\\)\n- \\(x_B \\approx 1.269\\)\n- \\(x_C \\approx 1.069\\)\n- \\(x_D \\approx 1.122\\)\n- \\(x_E \\approx 0.829\\)\n\nThe largest value of \\(x\\) is \\(x_B \\approx 1.269\\), which corresponds to equation \\(\\textbf{(B)}\\).\n\nTherefore, the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)}}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to determine which of the given equations has the largest solution for \\(x\\) when \\(0 < r < 3\\). Let's analyze each equation step by step.\n\n1. **Equation (A): \\(3(1 + r)^x = 7\\)**\n\n   Rearranging, we get:\n   \\[\n   (1 + r)^x = \\frac{7}{3}\n   \\]\n   Taking the natural logarithm on both sides:\n   \\[\n   x \\ln(1 + r) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(1 + r)}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Equation (B): \\(3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7\\)**\n\n   Rearranging, we get:\n   \\[\n   (1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = \\frac{7}{3}\n   \\]\n   Taking the natural logarithm on both sides:\n   \\[\n   x \\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\right) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\right)}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Equation (C): \\(3(1 + 2r)^x = 7\\)**\n\n   Rearranging, we get:\n   \\[\n   (1 + 2r)^x = \\frac{7}{3}\n   \\]\n   Taking the natural logarithm on both sides:\n   \\[\n   x \\ln(1 + 2r) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(1 + 2r)}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Equation (D): \\(3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7\\)**\n\n   Rearranging, we get:\n   \\[\n   (1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3}\n   \\]\n   Taking the natural logarithm on both sides:\n   \\[\n   x \\ln(1 + \\sqrt{r}) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(1 + \\sqrt{r})}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Equation (E): \\(3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7\\)**\n\n   Rearranging, we get:\n   \\[\n   (1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3}\n   \\]\n   Taking the natural logarithm on both sides:\n   \\[\n   x \\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right)}\n   \\]\n\nTo determine which of these expressions for \\(x\\) is the largest, we need to compare the denominators of each expression, since the numerator \\(\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\) is constant. The larger the denominator, the smaller the value of \\(x\\). Therefore, we need to compare the values of \\(\\ln(1 + r)\\), \\(\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\right)\\), \\(\\ln(1 + 2r)\\), \\(\\ln(1 + \\sqrt{r})\\), and \\(\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right)\\).\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), we can analyze the behavior of these logarithmic functions:\n\n- \\(\\ln(1 + r)\\) increases as \\(r\\) increases.\n- \\(\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\right)\\) increases but at a slower rate than \\(\\ln(1 + r)\\).\n- \\(\\ln(1 + 2r)\\) increases more rapidly than \\(\\ln(1 + r)\\).\n- \\(\\ln(1 + \\sqrt{r})\\) increases at a rate between \\(\\ln(1 + r)\\) and \\(\\ln(1 + 2r)\\).\n- \\(\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right)\\) decreases as \\(r\\) increases because \\(\\frac{1}{r}\\) decreases as \\(r\\) increases.\n\nTherefore, the smallest value among these logarithms will be \\(\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right)\\), which means the largest value of \\(x\\) will be obtained from the equation with \\(\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right)\\) in the denominator, which is equation (E).\n\nThus, the equation with the largest solution for \\(x\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the range of r\nr_values = np.linspace(0.01, 2.99, 1000)\n\n# Calculate the values of the denominators for each equation\nln_1_plus_r = np.log(1 + r_values)\nln_1_plus_r_10 = np.log(1 + r_values / 10)\nln_1_plus_2r = np.log(1 + 2 * r_values)\nln_1_plus_sqrt_r = np.log(1 + np.sqrt(r_values))\nln_1_plus_1_over_r = np.log(1 + 1 / r_values)\n\n# Find the maximum value for each denominator\nmax_ln_1_plus_r = np.max(ln_1_plus_r)\nmax_ln_1_plus_r_10 = np.max(ln_1_plus_r_10)\nmax_ln_1_plus_2r = np.max(ln_1_plus_2r)\nmax_ln_1_plus_sqrt_r = np.max(ln_1_plus_sqrt_r)\nmax_ln_1_plus_1_over_r = np.max(ln_1_plus_1_over_r)\n\n# Compare the maximum values of the denominators\nprint((max_ln_1_plus_r, max_ln_1_plus_r_10, max_ln_1_plus_2r, max_ln_1_plus_sqrt_r, max_ln_1_plus_1_over_r))\n```", "To determine which equation has the largest solution for \\(x\\), we need to solve each equation for \\(x\\) and compare the solutions. We can use logarithms to solve for \\(x\\). Here are the steps:\n\n1. Rewrite each equation in the form \\((1 + r')^x = \\frac{7}{3}\\), where \\(r'\\) is the effective rate for each equation.\n2. Use the logarithm to solve for \\(x\\): \\(x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + r')}\\).\n3. Compare the values of \\(x\\) for each equation.\n\nLet's solve this using Python and SymPy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the equations\nr = sp.symbols('r', positive=True, real=True)\nequations = [\n    3 * (1 + r)**x - 7,\n    3 * (1 + r/10)**x - 7,\n    3 * (1 + 2*r)**x - 7,\n    3 * (1 + sp.sqrt(r))**x - 7,\n    3 * (1 + 1/r)**x - 7\n]\n\n# Solve each equation for x\nsolutions = []\nfor eq in equations:\n    sol = sp.solve(eq, x)\n    solutions.append(sol[0])\n\n# Simplify the solutions\nsolutions = [sp.simplify(sol) for sol in solutions]\n\n# Print the solutions\nprint(solutions)\n```\n```output\n[log(7/3)/log(r + 1), log(7/3)/log(r/10 + 1), log(7/3)/log(2*r + 1), log(7/3)/log(sqrt(r) + 1), log(7/3)/log((r + 1)/r)]\n```\nThe output provides the solutions for \\(x\\) in terms of \\(r\\):\n\n1. \\(\\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(r + 1)}\\)\n2. \\(\\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log\\left(\\frac{r}{10} + 1\\right)}\\)\n3. \\(\\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(2r + 1)}\\)\n4. \\(\\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(\\sqrt{r} + 1)}\\)\n5. \\(\\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log\\left(\\frac{r + 1}{r}\\right)}\\)\n\nTo determine which equation has the largest solution for \\(x\\), we need to compare the denominators of these expressions. The larger the denominator, the smaller the value of \\(x\\), and vice versa. So, we need to compare \\(\\log(r + 1)\\), \\(\\log\\left(\\frac{r}{10} + 1\\right)\\), \\(\\log(2r + 1)\\), \\(\\log(\\sqrt{r} + 1)\\), and \\(\\log\\left(\\frac{r + 1}{r}\\right)\\).\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), let's analyze the behavior of these logarithms:\n\n1. \\(\\log(r + 1)\\) is the logarithm of a number between 1 and 4.\n2. \\(\\log\\left(\\frac{r}{10} + 1\\right)\\) is the logarithm of a number between 1 and 1.3.\n3. \\(\\log(2r + 1)\\) is the logarithm of a number between 1 and 7.\n4. \\(\\log(\\sqrt{r} + 1)\\) is the logarithm of a number between 1 and approximately 2.45.\n5. \\(\\log\\left(\\frac{r + 1}{r}\\right)\\) is the logarithm of a number between 1 and approximately 2.\n\nClearly, the smallest denominator is \\(\\log\\left(\\frac{r}{10} + 1\\right)\\), which means the largest value of \\(x\\) is obtained from the equation \\(3(1 + r/10)^x = 7\\).\n\nSo, the equation with the largest solution for \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{3(1 + r/10)^x = 7}\\).", "To determine which equation has the largest solution \\( x \\), we need to solve each equation for \\( x \\) and compare the solutions. The equations are:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{(A)} & \\quad 3(1 + r)^x = 7, \\\\\n\\text{(B)} & \\quad 3(1 + r/10)^x = 7, \\\\\n\\text{(C)} & \\quad 3(1 + 2r)^x = 7, \\\\\n\\text{(D)} & \\quad 3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7, \\\\\n\\text{(E)} & \\quad 3(1 + 1/r)^x = 7.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we can isolate the exponential term in each equation:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n(1 + r)^x &= \\frac{7}{3}, \\\\\n(1 + r/10)^x &= \\frac{7}{3}, \\\\\n(1 + 2r)^x &= \\frac{7}{3}, \\\\\n(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x &= \\frac{7}{3}, \\\\\n(1 + 1/r)^x &= \\frac{7}{3}.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSince the right-hand side of each equation is the same, the value of \\( x \\) will be determined by the base of the exponential term. The larger the base, the smaller the value of \\( x \\) will be, because a larger base will require a smaller exponent to reach the same value.\n\nSo, we need to compare the bases:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n1 + r, \\\\\n1 + r/10, \\\\\n1 + 2r, \\\\\n1 + \\sqrt{r}, \\\\\n1 + 1/r.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 0 < r < 3 \\), we can compare these bases directly. The largest base will give the smallest value of \\( x \\), and the smallest base will give the largest value of \\( x \\).\n\nLet's compare the bases:\n\n1. \\( 1 + r \\)\n2. \\( 1 + r/10 \\)\n3. \\( 1 + 2r \\)\n4. \\( 1 + \\sqrt{r} \\)\n5. \\( 1 + 1/r \\)\n\nSince \\( r \\) is a positive number less than 3, the smallest value of \\( r \\) is close to 0, and the largest value of \\( r \\) is close to 3. Let's evaluate the bases at the boundaries:\n\n- For \\( r \\) close to 0:\n  - \\( 1 + r \\approx 1 \\)\n  - \\( 1 + r/10 \\approx 1 \\)\n  - \\( 1 + 2r \\approx 1 \\)\n  - \\( 1 + \\sqrt{r} \\approx 1 \\)\n  - \\( 1 + 1/r \\approx 1 + \\infty = \\infty \\)\n\n- For \\( r \\) close to 3:\n  - \\( 1 + r = 1 + 3 = 4 \\)\n  - \\( 1 + r/10 = 1 + 3/10 = 1.3 \\)\n  - \\( 1 + 2r = 1 + 2 \\cdot 3 = 7 \\)\n  - \\( 1 + \\sqrt{r} = 1 + \\sqrt{3} \\approx 1 + 1.732 = 2.732 \\)\n  - \\( 1 + 1/r = 1 + 1/3 \\approx 1.333 \\)\n\nFrom these evaluations, we can see that \\( 1 + 2r \\) is the largest base when \\( r \\) is close to 3, and \\( 1 + 1/r \\) is the largest base when \\( r \\) is close to 0. However, since \\( r \\) is a positive number less than 3, the base \\( 1 + 2r \\) will be the largest for all values of \\( r \\) in the interval \\( 0 < r < 3 \\).\n\nTherefore, the equation with the largest solution \\( x \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3(1 + 2r)^x = 7}\n\\]", "Let's start by solving each equation for \\(x\\). We'll use the logarithm to isolate \\(x\\) in each equation.\n\n1. For equation (A): \\(3(1 + r)^x = 7\\)\n\\[ (1 + r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\]\n\\[ x \\log(1 + r) = \\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + r)} \\]\n\n2. For equation (B): \\(3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7\\)\n\\[ (1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\]\n\\[ x \\log\\left(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\right) = \\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log\\left(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\right)} \\]\n\n3. For equation (C): \\(3(1 + 2r)^x = 7\\)\n\\[ (1 + 2r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\]\n\\[ x \\log(1 + 2r) = \\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + 2r)} \\]\n\n4. For equation (D): \\(3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7\\)\n\\[ (1 + \\sqrt{r