[\n2 \\cdot 3 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) + 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\n\nWe know the values of \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\), \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}) = -\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values in, we get:\n\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow we can compute this step by step:\n\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nAdding these together, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given vectors\na_cross_b = np.array([6, -7, 3])\na_cross_c = np.array([4, 7, 2])\nb_cross_c = np.array([1, -7, 18])\n\n# Calculate the required cross product\nresult = 6 * b_cross_c + 2 * (-a_cross_b) - 3 * a_cross_c\nprint(result)\n```", "To solve for \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\), we can use the distributive property of the cross product:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = 2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} + 2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this expression using the properties of the cross product:\n\n1. \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}\\) (the cross product of a vector with itself is the zero vector).\n2. \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})\\).\n3. \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} = 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c})\\).\n\nSo the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} - 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow we can compute this step by step:\n\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nAdding these together:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given vectors\na_cross_b = np.array([6, -7, 3])\na_cross_c = np.array([4, 7, 2])\nb_cross_c = np.array([1, -7, 18])\n\n# Compute the expression\nresult = 6 * b_cross_c - 2 * a_cross_b - 3 * a_cross_c\nprint(result)\n```", 'To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product to expand this expression:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = 2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} + 2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}\\) and that the cross product is anti-commutative, so \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})\\) and \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = -(\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{a})\\). Thus, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\n\\[\n6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values, we get:\n\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} - 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow we can compute this step by step. First, we compute each scalar multiplication:\n\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow we add these vectors together:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]', 'To compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\), we will use the distributive property of the cross product over vector addition and scalar multiplication. Specifically, we will use the property that \\((\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) \\times \\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{u} \\times (\\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{w}) = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w}\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nWe need to compute:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}).\n\\]\n\nUsing the distributive property of the cross product, we get:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying each term, we get:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}),\n\\]\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = -2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}),\n\\]\n\\[\n(-\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) = -3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}),\n\\]\n\\[\n(-\\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}) - 