(x^2 + 2x - 3)}{(x + 1)^3} = \\frac{2x^2 + 4x + 2 - 2x^2 - 4x + 6}{(x + 1)^3} = \\frac{8}{(x + 1)^3}.\\]\n\nAt \\(x = 1\\):\n\n\\[f''(1) = \\frac{8}{(1 + 1)^3} = \\frac{8}{8} = 1 > 0,\\]\n\nwhich means the function has a local minimum at \\(x = 1\\).\n\nFinally, we evaluate the function at \\(x = 1\\):\n\n\\[f(1) = \\frac{(1 + 5)(1 + 2)}{1 + 1} = \\frac{6 \\cdot 3}{2} = \\frac{18}{2} = 9.\\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of the function \\(\\frac{(x + 5)(x + 2)}{x + 1}\\) for \\(x > 0\\) is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).", "To find the minimum value of the function \\(\\frac{(x + 5)(x + 2)}{x + 1}\\) for \\(x > 0\\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Define the function \\(f(x) = \\frac{(x + 5)(x + 2)}{x + 1}\\).\n2. Find the derivative of the function \\(f'(x)\\).\n3. Set the derivative equal to zero to find the critical points.\n4. Determine which of these critical points gives the minimum value by evaluating the second derivative or by evaluating the function at the critical points.\n5. Verify that the critical point is within the domain \\(x > 0\\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python using the sympy library:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the function\nf = (x + 5) * (x + 2) / (x + 1)\n\n# Compute the first derivative of the function\nf_prime = sp.diff(f, x)\n\n# Solve for critical points by setting the first derivative to zero\ncritical_points = sp.solve(f_prime, x)\n\n# Filter critical points to ensure they are in the domain x > 0\ncritical_points = [point for point in critical_points if point > 0]\n\n# Compute the second derivative of the function\nf_double_prime = sp.diff(f_prime, x)\n\n# Check the second derivative at the critical points to determine if they are minima\nmin_value = None\nfor point in critical_points:\n    if f_double_prime.subs(x, point) > 0:\n        min_value = f.subs(x, point)\n        break\n\nprint(min_value)\n```\n```output\nsqrt(6)*(2 + sqrt(6))*(5 + sqrt(6))/6\n```\nThe output provided i