ls Where the Denominator is Negative:**\n   The expression \\(x^2 - 36x = x(x - 36)\\) is a parabola that opens upwards and crosses the x-axis at \\(x = 0\\) and \\(x = 36\\). It is negative between these two points, i.e., in the interval \\(0 < x < 36\\).\n\n5. **Exclude the Points Where the Denominator is Zero:**\n   The fraction is undefined at \\(x = 0\\) and \\(x = 36\\), so these points must be excluded from the solution.\n\n6. **Combine the Results:**\n   The inequality \\(\\frac{|x^2 - 81|}{x^2 - 36x} < 0\\) holds true for \\(0 < x < 36\\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python and sympy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the inequality\nnumerator = sp.Abs(x**2 - 81)\ndenominator = x**2 - 36*x\ninequality = numerator / denominator < 0\n\n# Solve the inequality\nsolution = sp.solve_univariate_inequality(inequality, x, relational=False)\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\nInterval.open(0, 36)\n```\nThe solution to the inequality \\(\\frac{|x^2 - 81|}{x^2 - 36x} < 0\\) is the interval \\(0 < x < 36\\). Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, 36)}\n\\]", 'To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{|x^2 - 81|}{x^2 - 36x} < 0\\), we need to analyze the expression and determine the values of \\(x\\) that make the inequality true.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Analyze the Numerator:**\n   The numerator is \\(|x^2 - 81|\\). The absolute value function is always non-negative, so \\(|x^2 - 81| \\geq 0\\). The only time it is zero is when \\(x^2 - 81 = 0\\), which gives \\(x = 9\\) or \\(x = -9\\).\n\n2. **Analyze the Denominator:**\n   The denominator is \\(x^2 - 36x = x(x - 36)\\). The expression \\(x(x - 36)\\) is zero when \\(x = 0\\) or \\(x = 36\\). These values make the denominator zero and thus are not in the domain of the original expression.\n\n3. **Determine the Sign of the Expression:**\n   The inequality \\(\\frac{|x^2 - 81|}{x^2 - 36x} < 0\\) will be true when the denominator is negative because