1. A square piece (piece "e").\n2. Four isosceles right triangles.\n3. Three other shapes (which we don\'t need to consider for this problem).\n\nSince the square piece "e" is one of the pieces of the tangram, and the tangram is made up of 7 pieces that fit perfectly into the square, the area of the square piece "e" must be a fraction of the total area of the square. In a standard tangram, the square piece "e" is one of the 7 pieces, so it occupies \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) of the total area of the square.\n\nHowever, in this problem, the tangram pieces are slightly different, and we need to determine the area of the gray piece. The gray piece is one of the four isosceles right triangles. Each of these triangles is congruent, and together they make up half of the area of the square (since the other half is taken up by the square piece "e" and the other shapes).\n\nSo, the area of the gray piece is \\( \\frac{1}{4} \\) of the area of the square minus the area of the square piece "e". Since the square piece "e" is not specified to be a specific fraction of the total area in this problem, we will assume it is a standard tangram piece, which means it occupies \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) of the total area.\n\nLet\'s calculate the area of the gray piece:\n\n1. The area of the square is 16 square inches.\n2. The area of the square piece "e" is \\( \\frac{16}{7} \\) square inches.\n3. The remaining area for the four isosceles right triangles is \\( 16 - \\frac{16}{7} = \\frac{112}{7} - \\frac{16}{7} = \\frac{96}{7} \\) square inches.\n4. Since there are four isosceles right triangles, the area of each triangle is \\( \\frac{96/7}{4} = \\frac{24}{7} \\) square inches.\n\nSo, the area of the gray piece is \\( \\frac{24}{7} \\) square inches.\n\nLet\'s confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Total area of the square\r\ntotal_area = 16\r\n\r\n# Area of the square piece "e" (assuming it\'s 1/7 of the total area)\r\narea_e = total_area / 7\r\n\r\n# Remaining area for the four isosceles 