d the corresponding polynomial\nfor sol in solutions:\n    if sol.is_real and -1/3 <= sol <= 1/3:\n        # Find the polynomial for the root\n        poly = sp.Poly(sp.expand(sp.simplify((x - sol) * (x - sp.conjugate(sol)) * (x - sp.conjugate(sp.conjugate(sol))))), x)\n        a, b, c, d = poly.all_coeffs()\n        # Calculate the sum of the absolute values of the coefficients\n        result = abs(a) + abs(b) + abs(c) + abs(d)\n        print(f"Solution: {sol}, Polynomial: {poly}, Sum of coefficients: {result}")\n\n# The smallest possible value of |a| + |b| + |c| + |d|\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\nNo algorithms are implemented to solve equation 3*acos(3*x) + 2*acos(2*x) + acos(x) - pi\n```\nIt seems that the direct symbolic solution for the equation \\(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi\\) is not straightforward using sympy. Instead, we can use a numerical approach to find the value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation, and then use polynomial fitting to find the cubic polynomial.\n\nLet\'s use a numerical solver to find the value of \\(x\\), and then use polynomial fitting to find the cubic polynomial.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import fsolve\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols(\'x\')\n\n# Define the equation\neq = sp.acos(x) + sp.acos(2*x) + sp.acos(3*x) - sp.pi\n\n# Convert the sympy equation to a numerical function\nf = sp.lambdify(x, eq, \'numpy\')\n\n# Use fsolve to find the numerical solution\nsol = fsolve(f, 0.1)[0]\n\n# Now we need to find the cubic polynomial for this solution\n# We assume the solution is a root of a cubic polynomial ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0\n# We will use the fact that the sum of the roots of the polynomial is -b/a, etc.\n\n# Let\'s assume the polynomial is of the form ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0\n# We will use the fact that sol is a root of this polynomial\n# We will use polynomial fitting to find the coefficients a, b, c, d\n\n# We need at least 4 points to fit a cubic p