angle P$ because they are corresponding angles. Therefore, $\\angle STR = 40^\\circ$.\n\nHowever, we need to verify this using the angles around point $S$. The angle $\\angle STR$ can also be found by subtracting $\\angle Q$ from $180^\\circ$ and then subtracting $\\angle P$ from the result. This is because the sum of angles around a point on a straight line is $180^\\circ$.\n\nLet's calculate it step-by-step using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given angles\nangle_P = 40\nangle_Q = 35\n\n# Since ST is parallel to QR, angle STR is equal to angle P\nangle_STR = angle_P\n\n# Alternatively, we can calculate it as follows:\n# The angle STR can be found by considering the angles around point S\n# The angle STR is the same as the angle P because of the parallel lines and the transversal\n# Therefore, angle STR = angle P = 40 degrees\n\nprint(angle_STR)\n```\n```output\n40\n```\nThe measure of $\\angle STR$ is $\\boxed{75^\\circ}$.", "Given the diagram, we know the following:\n\n1. $\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}$\n2. $\\angle P = 40^\\circ$\n3. $\\angle Q = 35^\\circ$\n\nWe need to find the measure of $\\angle STR$.\n\nSince $\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}$, we know that $\\angle STR$ is equal to $\\angle Q$ because they are corresponding angles. This is a property of parallel lines and transversals.\n\nSo, $\\angle STR = \\angle Q = 35^\\circ$.\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code for clarity, although this is a straightforward geometric property.\n\nWe can simply print the value of $\\angle Q$ to confirm the measure of $\\angle STR$.\n```python\n# Given angles\nangle_P = 40\nangle_Q = 35\n\n# Since ST is parallel to QR, angle STR is equal to angle Q\nangle_STR = angle_Q\n\n# Output the result\nprint(angle_STR)\n```\n```output\n35\n```\nThe measure of $\\angle STR$ is \\(\\boxed{35}\\) degrees.", 'Since $\\overline{ST}\\parallel\\overline{QR}$, we have $\\angle STR = \\angle QRP$. Since $\\angle P = 40^\\circ$ and $\\angle Q = 35^\\circ$,