pression is not defined for 2 values of \\( x \\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", 'To determine for how many values of \\( x \\) the expression \\(\\frac{x-5}{x^2-9}\\) is not defined, we need to identify the values of \\( x \\) that make the denominator equal to zero. The expression is not defined when the denominator is zero.\n\nThe denominator of the expression is \\( x^2 - 9 \\). We can factor this as \\( (x - 3)(x + 3) \\). So, the expression is not defined when \\( x = 3 \\) or \\( x = -3 \\).\n\nTherefore, there are 2 values of \\( x \\) for which the expression is not defined.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).', 'To determine for how many values of \\(x\\) the expression \\(\\frac{x-5}{x^2-9}\\) is not defined, we need to find the values of \\(x\\) that make the denominator equal to zero. The expression is not defined when the denominator is zero, as division by zero is undefined.\n\nThe denominator of the expression is \\(x^2 - 9\\). We can factor this as \\((x - 3)(x + 3)\\). So, the expression is not defined when \\(x - 3 = 0\\) or \\(x + 3 = 0\\). Solving these equations, we get \\(x = 3\\) and \\(x = -3\\). Therefore, the expression is not defined for \\(x = 3\\) and \\(x = -3\\).\n\nThus, there are 2 values of \\(x\\) for which the expression is not defined. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).', 'To determine for how many values of \\( x \\) the expression \\(\\frac{x-5}{x^2-9}\\) is not defined, we need to identify the values of \\( x \\) that make the denominator equal to zero. The expression is not defined when the denominator is zero.\n\nThe denominator is \\( x^2 - 9 \\). We can factor this as \\( (x - 3)(x + 3) \\). Setting this equal to zero, we get:\n\\[ x^2 - 9 = 0 \\]\n\\[ (x - 3)(x + 3) = 0 \\]\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[ x - 3 = 0 \\implies x = 3 \\]\n\\[ x + 3 = 0 \\implies x = -3 \\]\n\nSo, the expression \\(\\frac{x-5}{x^2-9}\\) is not defined for \\( x = 3 \\) and \\( x = -3 \\). Therefore, there are 2 values