= \\frac{10}{10} \\cdot 6 = 6, \\quad d = \\frac{10}{10} \\cdot 8 = 8,\n\\]\nbut scaled down proportionally to satisfy \\(a + b + c + d = 10\\). The correct values are:\n\\[\na = \\frac{10}{10} \\cdot 2 = 2, \\quad b = \\frac{10}{10} \\cdot 4 = 4, \\quad c = \\frac{10}{10} \\cdot 6 = 6, \\quad d = \\frac{10}{10} \\cdot 8 = 8,\n\\]\nbut scaled down proportionally to", "To find the class average (mean) on the test, we need to calculate the total score of all students and then divide it by the number of students.\n\nHere's the step-by-step process:\n\n1. Calculate the total score of the students who scored 80.\n2. Calculate the total score of the students who scored 90.\n3. Calculate the total score of the students who scored 100.\n4. Sum these total scores to get the overall total score of the class.\n5. Divide the overall total score by the total number of students (30) to get the class average.\n\nLet's implement this in Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Number of students and their scores\nstudents_80 = 20\nstudents_90 = 8\nstudents_100 = 2\n\n# Total scores\ntotal_score_80 = students_80 * 80\ntotal_score_90 = students_90 * 90\ntotal_score_100 = students_100 * 100\n\n# Overall total score\noverall_total_score = total_score_80 + total_score_90 + total_score_100\n\n# Total number of students\ntotal_students = students_80 + students_90 + students_100\n\n# Class average\nclass_average = overall_total_score / total_students\nprint(class_average)\n```\n```output\n84.0\n```\nThe class average (mean) on the test is \\(\\boxed{84.0}\\).", "To find the class average (mean) on the test, we need to calculate the total score of all the students and then divide it by the total number of students. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. Calculate the total score for the students who scored 80:\n\\[ 20 \\text{ students} \\times 80 \\text{ points each} = 1600 \\text{ points} \\]\n\n2. Calculate the total score for the students who scored 90:\n\\[ 8 \\text{ students} \\times 90 \\text{ points each} = 720 \\text{ points} \\]\n\n3. Calculate the total score for the students who scored 100:\n\\[ 2 \\text{ students} \\times 100 \\text{ points each} = 200 \\text{ points} \\]\n\n4. Add up all the total scores to get the overall total score:\n\\[ 1600 + 720 + 200 = 2520 \\text{ points} \\]\n\n5. Divide the total score by the total number of students to get the average:\n\\[ \\frac{2520 \\text{ points}}{30 \\text{ students}} = 84 \\text{ points} \\]\n\nSo, the class average on the test is \\( \\boxed{84} \\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code.\n```python\n# Number of students and their scores\nstudents_80 = 20\nstudents_90 = 8\nstudents_100 = 2\n\n# Total score calculation\ntotal_score = (students_80 * 80) + (students_90 * 90) + (students_100 * 100)\n\n# Total number of students\ntotal_students = students_80 + students_90 + students_100\n\n# Class average calculation\nclass_average = total_score / total_students\nprint(class_average)\n```", "To find the class average (mean) on the test, we need to calculate the total score of all the students and then divide it by the number of students. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. Calculate the total score for the students who scored 80. Since 20 students scored 80, the total score for these students is \\(20 \\times 80\\).\n2. Calculate the total score for the students who scored 90. Since 8 students scored 90, the total score for these students is \\(8 \\times 90\\).\n3. Calculate the total score for the students who scored 100. Since 2 students scored 100, the total score for these students is \\(2 \\times 100\\).\n4. Sum these total scores to get the overall total score for the class.\n5. Divide the overall total score by the total number of students (30) to get the class average.\n\nLet's calculate this using Python code.\n```python\n# Number of students and their scores\nstudents_80 = 20\nstudents_90 = 8\nstudents_100 = 2\n\n# Total scores\ntotal_score_80 = students_80 * 80\ntotal_score