nomial of degree 8, and the right-hand side is a polynomial of degree 9. By comparing the degrees, we can see that the sum of the roots of the polynomial equation will be determined by the coefficient of \\(x^8\\) and \\(x^9\\).\n\nHowever, since the equation is quite complex, we can use a more direct approach by recognizing that the sum of the roots of the polynomial equation can be found by considering the symmetry and properties of the rational functions involved.\n\nGiven the symmetry in the equation, the sum of the roots will be zero because the equation is essentially a balance of positive and negative contributions from the rational functions on the left-hand side, and the linear function on the right-hand side does not affect the symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the sum of all complex solutions of the equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]", "To solve the given equation \\(\\frac{1}{x^2 - 1} + \\frac{2}{x^2 - 2} + \\frac{3}{x^2 - 3} + \\frac{4}{x^2 - 4} = 2010x - 4\\), we can start by letting \\(y = x^2\\). This substitution simplifies the equation to \\(\\frac{1}{y - 1} + \\frac{2}{y - 2} + \\frac{3}{y - 3} + \\frac{4}{y - 4} = 2010x - 4\\).\n\nFirst, we will find the common denominator for the left-hand side of the equation. The common denominator is \\((y-1)(y-2)(y-3)(y-4)\\). We can rewrite the left-hand side as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(y-2)(y-3)(y-4) + 2(y-1)(y-3)(y-4) + 3(y-1)(y-2)(y-4) + 4(y-1)(y-2)(y-3)}{(y-1)(y-2)(y-3)(y-4)} = 2010x - 4.\n\\]\n\nLet's simplify the numerator:\n\n\\[\n(y-2)(y-3)(y-4) + 2(y-1)(y-3)(y-4) + 3(y-1)(y-2)(y-4) + 4(y-1)(y-2)(y-3).\n\\]\n\nExpanding each term, we get:\n\n\\[\n(y-2)(y-3)(y-4) = y^3 - 9y^2 + 26y - 24,\n\\]\n\\[\n2(y-1)(y-3)(y-4) = 2(y^3 - 8y^2 + 19y - 12) = 2y^3 - 16y^2 + 38y - 24,\n\\]\n\\[\n3(y-1)(y-2)(y-4) = 3(y^3 - 7y^2 + 14y - 8) = 3y^3 - 21y^2 + 42y - 24,\n\\]\n\\[\n4(y-1)(y-2)(y-3) = 4(y^3 - 6y^2 + 11y - 6) = 4y^3 - 24y^2 + 44y - 24.\n\\]\n\nAdding these together, we get:\n\n\\[\ny^3 - 9y^2 + 26y - 24 + 2y^3 - 16y^2 + 38y 