} & -\\frac{11}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{19}{2} & 3 - \\lambda & -\\frac{13}{2} \\\\\n-1 & 3 & -\\frac{13}{2} - \\lambda \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\right) = 0$$\nExpanding the determinant, we obtain a cubic equation in $\\lambda$. Solving this equation using numerical methods, we find the approximate eigenvalues as stated in the answer.\n####\nThe eigenvalues of the given matrix are approximately -3.621, 3.811 - 6.442i, and 3.811 + 6.442i.\n\nFind the sum of the following two polynomials: $p(x) = 11 x^2+\\frac{23 x}{4}-\\frac{25}{2}$, $q(x) = \\frac{1}{4} \\left(49 x^2+30 x-34\\right)$\nTo find the sum of the two polynomials, we can simply add the like terms together.\n\n$$p(x) + q(x) = (11 x^2+\\frac{23 x}{4}-\\frac{25}{2}) + (\\frac{1}{4} \\left(49 x^2+30 x-34\\right))$$\n\n$$= 11 x^2+\\frac{23 x}{4}-\\frac{25}{2} + \\frac{49 x^2}{4}+\\frac{30 x}{4}-\\frac{34}{4}$$\n\n$$= (11 x^2+\\frac{49 x^2}{4})+(\\frac{23 x}{4}+\\frac{30 x}{4})+(\\frac{-25}{2}-\\frac{34}{4})$$\n\n$$= \\frac{93 x^2}{4}+\\frac{53 x}{4}-\\frac{44}{2}$$\n\n$$= \\frac{93 x^2}{4}+\\frac{53 x}{4}-22$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the two polynomials is $\\frac{93 x^2}{4}+\\frac{53 x}{4}-22$.\n####\nThe answer is $\\frac{93 x^2}{4}+\\frac{53 x}{4}-22$\n\nCompute the range of the set $\\left\\{\\frac{19}{2}, \\frac{19}{2}, 3, -4, \\frac{13}{\\sqrt{2}}, -2, -4 \\sqrt{5}, -3, 3, -\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{2}}, 0, -\\frac{16}{\\sqrt{3}}, -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}, 5 \\sqrt{3}, 1\\right\\}$.\nThe range of a set is the difference between the maximum and minimum values in the set. First, we need to identify these values.\n\nThe maximum value is $\\frac{19}{2}$, and the minimum value is $-\\frac{16}{\\sqrt{3}}$ (since $-\\frac{16}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is less than $-4\\sqrt{5}$).\n\nSo, the range is calculated as follows:\n\nRange = Maximum Value - Minimum Value\nRange = $\\frac{19}{2} - \\left(-\\frac{16}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)$\nRange = $\\frac{19}{2} + \\frac{16}{\\sqrt{3}}$\n\nHowever, to make the answer more readable, we can rationalize the denominator:\n\nRange = $\\frac{19}{2} + \\frac{16\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given set is $\\frac{19}{2} + \\frac{16\\sqrt{3}}{3}$.\n\nDetermine the magnitude (norm) and the argument (phase angle in radians) of the complex number $-\\frac{20 \\left(\\sin \\left(\\frac{13 \\pi }{60}\\right)+i \\cos \\left(\\frac{13 \\pi }{60}\\right)\\right)}{\\pi }$.\nThe magnitude (norm) of a complex number $a + bi$ is given by $\\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$. For the given complex number $-\\frac{20 \\left(\\sin \\left(\\frac{13 \\pi }{60}\\right)+i \\cos \\left(\\frac{13 \\pi }{60}\\right)\\right)}{\\pi }$, we have $a = -\\frac{20 \\sin \\left(\\frac{13 \\pi }{60}\\right)}{\\pi }$ and $b = -\\frac{20 \\cos \\left(\\frac{13 \\pi }{60}\\right)}{\\pi }$. Therefore, the norm is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Norm} = \\sqrt{\\left(-\\frac{20 \\sin \\left(\\frac{13 \\pi }{60}\\right)}{\\pi }\\right)^2 + \\left(-\\frac{20 \\cos \\left(\\frac{13 \\pi }{60}\\right)}{\\pi }\\right)^2} = \\frac{20 \\sqrt{\\sin ^2\\left(\\frac{13 \\pi }{60}\\right)+\\cos ^2\\left(\\frac{13 \\pi }{60}\\right)}}{\\pi } = \\frac{20}{\\pi }\n$$\n\nThe argument (phase angle in radians) is the angle that the complex number makes with the positive real axis. Since the original number is negative, the angle will be the opposite of the angle formed by the positive equivalent. The argument of the positive equivalent is $\\frac{13 \\pi }{60}$, so the argument for the negative number is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Argument} = -\\frac{13 \\pi }{60}\n$$\n\nHowever, to express the angle in the standard range $(-\\pi, \\pi]$, we need to adjust the angle:\n\n$$\n\\text{Argument} = -\\frac{13 \\pi }{60} + \\pi = \\frac{7 \\pi }{60}\n$$\n\nThus, the norm is $\\frac{20}{\\pi }$ and the argument is $\\frac{7 \\pi }{60}$.\n\nFind the second-order Taylor expansion of the function $f(x) = \\frac{2401 x^4}{81}$ around the point $x = 2$.\nTo find the second-order Taylor expansion of a function $f(x)$ around a point $a$, we use the formula:\n\n$$f(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2$$\n\nwhere $f'(x)$ and $f''(x)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$, respectively.\n\nIn this case, $a = 2$, so we have:\n\n$$f(2) = \\frac{2401 (2)^4}{81} = 2401$$\n\n$$f'(x) = \\frac{d}{dx} \\left[ \\frac{2401 x^4}{81} \\right] = \\frac{2401 \\cdot 4x^3}{81} = \\frac{9604 x^3}{81}$$\n\n$$f'(2) = \\frac{9604 (2)^3}{81} = \\frac{38416}{81}$$\n\n$$f''(x) = \\frac{d}{dx} \\left[ \\frac{9604 x^3}{81} \\right] = \\frac{9604 \\cdot 3x^2}{81} = \\frac{28812 x^2}{81}$$\n\n$$f''(2) = \\frac{28812 (2)^2}{81} = \\frac{115248}{81}$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n$$f(x) = 2401 + \\frac{38416}{81} (x-2) + \\frac{115248}{162} (x-2)^2$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$$f(x) = \\frac{117649}{81} (x-2)^2 + \\frac{9604 (x-2)}{81} + \\frac{2401}{81}$$\n####\nThe answer is $$f(x) = \\frac{2401}{81} (x-2)^4 + \\frac{9604}{81} (x-2)^3 + \\frac{117649}{81} (x-2)^2 + \\frac{9604 (x-2)}{81} + \\frac{2401}{81}$$\n\nDetermine the number of electrons transferred in the following redox reaction:\n\n{eq}2MnO_4^- + 5SO_2 + 2H_2O \\to 2Mn^{2+} + 5SO_4^{2-} + 4H^+\n\n{/eq}\nThe oxidation state of Mn in {eq}MnO_4^-\n\n{/eq} is +7, while in {eq}Mn^{2+}\n\n{/eq} it is +2. This means that Mn loses 5 electrons. Therefore, the number of electrons transferred in the reaction is 5.\n\nSimplify the expression $\\left(3 \\sqrt{2} \\left(\\cos \\left(\\frac{11}{45}\\right)+i \\sin \\left(\\frac{11}{45}\\right)\\right)\\right)^2$.\nThe simplified form of the expression is $18 \\left(\\cos^2 \\left(\\frac{11}{45}\\right) + i^2 \\sin^2 \\left(\\frac{11}{45}\\right)\\right)$, which further simplifies to $18 \\left(\\cos \\left(\\frac{22}{45}\\right) + i \\sin \\left(\\frac{22}{45}\\right)\\right)$, as $i^2 = -1$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1$.\n\nFind the least squares solution to the linear system given by the matrix\n$\\left(\n\\begin{array}{cc}\n-3 & -2 \\\\\n-3 & 2 \\\\\n-2 & 3 \\\\\n-1 & 3 \\\\\n-3 & 3 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\