t_u * u[1]]\n\n# Test the projection for a vector v on the line y = -2x\nv = [1, -2]\nprojection = proj_u(v)\nprint(projection)\n```\n```output\n[0.0, 0.0]\n```\nThe projection of the vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$ onto the vector $\\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ is indeed the zero vector, confirming that the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ lies on the line $y = -2x$.\n\nThus, the equation of the line on which the vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ lie is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = -2x}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to understand the projection of a vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) onto another vector \\(\\mathbf{a}\\). The projection of \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) onto \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}}{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}} \\mathbf{a}\n\\]\nIn this problem, \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and we want the projection to be the zero vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\). This means that the dot product \\(\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}\\) must be zero, because if the dot product is zero, the projection will be zero.\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}\\). The dot product \\(\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 2x + y\n\\]\nSetting this equal to zero gives us the equation:\n\\[\n2x + y = 0\n\\]\nSolving for \\(y\\) in terms of \\(x\\), we get:\n\\[\ny = -2x\n\\]\nSo the equation of the line is \\(y = -2x\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx, y = sp.symbols('x y')\n\n# Define the vector a\na = sp.Matrix([2, 1])\n\n# Define the vector v\nv = sp.Matrix([x, y])\n\n# Calculate the dot product of v and a\ndot_product = v.dot(a)\n\n# Solve the equation dot_product = 0 for y\nsolution = sp.solve(dot_product, y)\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n[-2